faqs:
  - q: "Do you use AI to generate or manipulate your images?"
    a: "Absolutely not. Rafa is an editorial photographer first; he works under the photojournalism code of non-manipulation. Every image is a real moment, captured on location. Adjustments are limited to what could be done in a traditional darkroom: exposure, contrast, color balance, dodging, burning. Nothing is added, removed, or generated. If you need our work for editorial use, you can rely on it being authentic."

  - q: "Do you travel internationally for projects?"
    a: "Yes, we're based in Barcelona but routinely work across Europe, Latin America, California, and beyond. Our overlanding setup means we can reach locations standard production crews can't access on short notice. That said, it doesn't always have to involve the Defender. For many NGO projects we've travelled light: the whole family, just Rafa, or just Mariella, depending on what the assignment needs."

  - q: "Do you shoot video as well as stills?"
    a: "Photography is Rafa's main medium and our first love, but yes, we do video too. Rafa actually trained as a film director, so the moving image isn't a side hustle for us. We also handle immersive 360/VR through our Reality Telling work. Tell us what you need and we'll tell you the right format for it."

  - q: "Can we license existing images from your expeditions?"
    a: "Yes. Image licensing is Rafa's world actually, he runs a photo agency and licenses images for a living. Get in touch with what you have in mind and we'll figure it out."

  - q: "Do you work with NGOs at adjusted rates?"
    a: "We do. Our nonprofit work is core to what we do, and Mariella brings deep expertise from the NGO world: project writing, identifying funding from public and private calls, and structuring proposals that get backed. So we can often help shape the funding side as well as the storytelling. Reach out and let's talk scope and budget."

  - q: "How does the Reality Telling 360/VR service work?"
    a: "We capture immersive 360-degree footage on location and produce VR-ready experiences for healthcare, cultural, and impact contexts. The story behind it: before The Defender Family, this was a side family project. We started experimenting with 360 content, and it grew into a big part of Reality Telling because patients respond so strongly to immersive experiences. That response is what led us to create the dedicated Travel section, Moments, and of course <a href=\"/sophie-on-board/\" style=\"color: var(--color-warm-dark); text-decoration: underline;\">Sophie on Board</a>, curated specifically for kids. Best discussed on a call given the production specifics."

  - q: "Are you interested in collaborations with brands?"
    a: "If we're the right fit for each other, absolutely. We're not the right partner for everyone, and that's the point, collaborations work when the brand and the story belong to the same world."

  - q: "Can we buy prints?"
    a: "Of course. Reach out with the image you have in mind and we'll quote you on size, paper, and edition."

  - q: "What's your minimum project scope?"
    a: "We don't have a hard minimum, but most engagements start at multi-day field shoots. For single-day editorial assignments, get in touch and we'll tell you if we're the right fit."
---

